From: well0nez <well0nez@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] drm: h713: show the HDMI capture ring on the video plane

The video plane this driver gained with the fullscreen NV12 overlay is
nailed to 1280x720 NV12 with a chroma stride equal to the luma stride. That
is the right description of a Cedrus frame. It is not a description of
anything this projector can actually show today: the one live video source
is the HDMI receiver, the MIPS firmware captures it as NV16 at the panel's
1920x1080, and on a 1920x1080 board the plane's atomic_check rejects every
framebuffer, so the plane is listed and unusable.

Four things change.

Geometry comes from the framebuffer instead of from a constant. The two
geometry words are computed, and both formulas reproduce the two register
sets that are known good byte for byte: 0x02cf04ff/0x002c004f at 1280x720,
which this driver wrote before, and 0x043f077f/0x00420077 at 1920x1080,
which the firmware programs for the HDMI capture on the HY310. The crop
registers +0x048/+0x04c are now written too; they used to be left at
whatever the firmware had put there, which happened to be right on one
board and is not a description.

NV16 is added to the format list, and it is fed to the hardware without
switching the format code. Source 0 has a linear NV12 reader: for output
line r it fetches chroma line r/2. NV16 carries one chroma line per picture
line, so the colour comes out stretched 2x vertically -- anchored at the
top, colour from the upper half of the picture lying over the lower half.
Doubling the chroma stride puts chroma line r/2 at offset r/2 * 2 * pitch,
which is picture line r: exact 4:2:2 out of an NV12 reader, one register,
no format switch. Switching the format code instead (0x03000213) breaks the
luma geometry and turns the picture green. Everything else follows: the
format field in CTRL stays 0 and the chroma crop keeps the NV12 height h/2,
because that is what the reader is being told it is reading.

The plane can source the capture ring itself, under a new "hdmi-ring"
property. This is transitional -- it goes away when the capture driver hands
the three ring slots out as dma-bufs -- but without it the plane has nothing
to show. The firmware rotates a pair of flip pointers at +0x320/+0x324
through the ring at the capture's 60 Hz, and the pair always names one slot
index, so the vsync handler reads it and hands it to the scanout queue. The
pointers are read, never written; the pool-2 control words next to them
ignore CPU writes anyway.

The VidDec frame descriptor is published on enable, exactly once, and is not
withdrawn on disable. Every change to that record is a signal event to the
firmware: it re-runs its WCE path and reprograms stride and mode registers
behind the driver's back. Writing it a second time froze the picture and
left the capture wedged until the next cold start; why it does not recover
is still open. A content comparison makes the publish idempotent, so a
module reload does not re-arm it either, and the pointer is left in the
queue while source 0 is off so that re-enabling needs no second publish.

That signal event is also the reason the chroma stride has to be programmed
after the descriptor rather than before it. The WCE reprograms source 0 out of
the record, and everything it computes there is what this plane writes anyway:
geometry 0x043f077f/0x00420077, crop 0x04380780/0x021c0780, luma stride 0x780.
The record carries a single stride, though, so it puts the luma pitch into the
chroma stride as well -- and that is the one value this plane deliberately does
not take from the record. On hardware the plane therefore came up with a halved
chroma stride out of exactly the two prior states in which the pointer was not
already standing, a cold start and a run after the bring-up script had cleared
it, and with the right one out of every state in which it was standing --
including a chroma stride poked to the same wrong value by hand immediately
before the enable. Six runs, three outcomes, one variable; nothing else in the
register set differs, because nothing else in the register set disagrees with
the record.

So the pointer words move into the publish, where they are written once instead
of on every commit, and the enable brackets the event: it parks a zero in the
chroma stride, which is not a stride the WCE can produce, and waits for the WCE
to replace it before programming source 0. That is for NV16 only -- for NV12
the WCE computes the stride the plane wants anyway, so there is nothing to lose
and nothing to wait for. The bring-up recipe has always had this separation,
publishing the descriptor and configuring source 0 as two separate steps, which
is why the same chroma stride sticks there.

The wait is one second, which is the bound the board actually gave: on the cold
start that produced the trace above, the descriptor write, the firmware's
answer and the driver's source-0 programming all fell into a single one-second
sampling interval, and nothing has been measured more finely than that. Sixty
hertz suggests the answer really arrives in about seventeen milliseconds, but a
budget picked from what the firmware ought to do rather than from what it was
seen to do would end the wait while the firmware is still entitled to answer,
and the result would be precisely the halved chroma stride this bracket is for.
It is at most one wait per boot, because the publication is only ever new once.
Running out is reported as an error by the caller rather than swallowed inside
the wait: the caller is the one that knows source 0 is about to be programmed
regardless, and the acceptance run reads that line.

Disable puts the chroma stride back to the value read at probe. Every other
piece of source-0 state this plane changes is restored there from a remembered
value, CTRL and the chroma gain and the RGB channel and the downstream
selector, and this one was not. Nothing downstream reads it while RGB owns the
output, but a plane that failed to program it inherited the previous cycle's
doubled value and looked correct -- which is how a broken first enable passed
an acceptance run.

The descriptor page moves out of the DMA pool for the same reason. It has a
second reader that is not a bus master: the MIPS reads it through its own
DRAM window, where ARM 0x4bxxxxxx appears at 0x8bxxxxxx. System CMA sits at
0x7dc00000 on this board and does not appear in that window at all. So the
page comes from a reserved region named in DT, split off the vendor's
128 KiB decoder buffer at the address stock parks the record at, and the
capture ring is named there as well -- the driver needs its bounds to be
sure a pointer it read out of the hardware really is a capture slot before
it hands it to scanout. Neither region is a DMA pool, so the existing
scanout override is looked up by name now; with no "scanout" entry present,
framebuffers keep coming from system CMA exactly as before.

Finally the chroma gain in PROC +0x508 becomes a "saturation" plane
property. 0x4c is the value the stock picture pipeline calibrates for user
saturation 50, and it stays the default; only bits [23:16] of the register
are replaced, the rest of the word is the stock HDMI value as before.

Last, and not about this plane at all: the flip pointers become a kernel
interface. They are the only "a capture slot is finished" event the ARM can
observe on this SoC -- there is no capture interrupt on this side, only the
MIPS has one (doku/84, RE question K2) -- so the V4L2 capture driver needs
them too, and it had asked for them with a reg of its own: eight bytes at
0x05600320, inside the 0x400 window this node claims. That does not work.
/proc/iomem reads "05600000-056003ff : 5600000.display afbd", so
devm_ioremap_resource() on those eight bytes returns -EBUSY, and it would be
the wrong shape even where it did work: two drivers sampling one rotating
pointer pair, each with its own torn-pair handling, one of them from a timer
that knows nothing about the vsync.

So the pair is handed out from here instead, as
include/linux/soc/sunxi/h713-afbd.h: h713_afbd_register_flip_notifier() puts
a consumer on an atomic notifier chain that the vsync handler calls with the
pair it has just seen change, and h713_afbd_read_flip() answers the questions
that cannot wait for a vsync -- "is there a signal at all", which V4L2 asks
while nothing is streaming. The chain is atomic and static: the handler runs
in hard interrupt context, so the path allocates nothing and takes no mutex,
and a consumer can still unregister after this driver has been unbound. The
event carries the raw pair and no slot index, because the slot table is
measured knowledge of the capture, while this driver knows only the bounds of
the region the ring lives in.

The vsync interrupt therefore has two users now, DRM's vblank machinery and
the notifier, and the mask is derived from both instead of from vblank alone.
With neither, nothing changes: the vsync handler does not so much as read the
pointer pair, and an idle system pays exactly what it paid before.

Signed-off-by: well0nez <well0nez@users.noreply.github.com>
